---
import Site from '../../layouts/Site.astro';

const jsonLd = {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Climate Risk at Raincoat LLC · Case Study',
    description: "How I contributed to Raincoat's parametric insurance climate pipelines: data acquisition, new perils, dashboards, and a modular architecture roadmap.",
    url: 'https://skarazdata.com/projects/case-study-raincoat',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Stéphane Karasiewicz', url: 'https://skarazdata.com' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Skaraz Data' },
    datePublished: '2026-05-21',
    inLanguage: 'en',
};

const changes = [
    ['Perils in production', '3 (Precipitation, Hurricane Wind, Earthquake), Drought early-stage', '4 (+ Water Balance), Heat Stress pipeline ready'],
    ['Dashboards I built', '0', '3 (Precipitation, Water Balance, Drought)'],
    ['Coverage', 'Caribbean + Colombia', 'Most of South America + 4 African countries'],
    ['Sales maps', 'Manual, on request', 'Country-level maps generated systematically'],
];
const stack = [
    ['Climate data', 'CHIRPS · IMERG · ERA5-Land · Copernicus CDS · WEkEO · NetCDF · Xarray'],
    ['Engineering', 'Python · Pandas · NumPy · scikit-learn · PostgreSQL · Docker · Kubernetes · CI/CD · GitHub Actions'],
    ['Frontend', 'Plotly Dash'],
    ['Methods', 'Time-series analysis · Calibration · Threshold modelling · Anomaly detection · Geospatial aggregation'],
];
---

<Site
    title="Case study · climate pipelines at Raincoat · Skaraz Data"
    description="Two years embedded at Raincoat, a parametric insurtech: satellite data acquisition, new perils (Water Balance, Heat Stress), dashboards, and a modular architecture roadmap."
    jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
    <section class="panel on-panel cs-head">
        <div class="wrap">
            <a class="back" href="/">← Home</a>
            <div class="cs-grid">
                <div class="cs-copy">
                    <p class="label label--trigger">Case study · embedded senior data scientist · Feb 2024 to Jan 2026</p>
                    <h1>Climate pipelines at Raincoat, a parametric insurtech.</h1>
                </div>
                <dl class="fields cs-fields">
                    <dt>Client</dt><dd>Raincoat LLC, parametric insurance for natural catastrophes</dd>
                    <dt>Markets</dt><dd>LatAm, the Caribbean, and Africa</dd>
                    <dt>Role</dt><dd>Satellite and reanalysis data layer, new perils, dashboards</dd>
                    <dt>Length</dt><dd>2 years, remote</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap two">
            <div class="prose">
                <p class="label label--index">The client</p>
                <p>Raincoat builds parametric insurance products for natural catastrophe risks: policies that pay out automatically when predefined climate or weather thresholds are triggered, across emerging markets in LatAm, the Caribbean and beyond.</p>
                <p>When I joined in February 2024, the company had already shipped three perils to production: Precipitation, Hurricane Wind and Earthquake, with Drought in early development. The codebase had grown peril by peril, and coverage was concentrated in the Caribbean and Colombia.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose">
                <p class="label label--index">The brief</p>
                <p><strong>Peril ceiling.</strong> Sales conversations kept surfacing needs the existing perils did not cover: drought intensity, agricultural water stress, heat. New perils took months, because each one lived in its own block of code.</p>
                <p><strong>Geographic ceiling.</strong> The team was extending from the Caribbean and Colombia toward the rest of South America and Africa. Adding a country was a config change; the real cost was how much data the system processed per client.</p>
                <p>My role was not to refactor the system. It was to plug in as a senior climate data scientist, extend coverage, ship new perils, and start showing what a more efficient architecture could look like.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tint">
        <div class="wrap">
            <div class="section-head reveal"><p class="label label--index">What I contributed</p><h2>Data acquisition, two new perils, three dashboards.</h2></div>
            <div class="grid cols-2">
                <div class="card reveal" style="--i:0"><p class="label label--trigger">Data acquisition</p><h3>Every peril except Hurricane Wind</h3><p class="muted">Ingestion logic for CHIRPS, ERA5-Land, IMERG, Copernicus CDS and WEkEO, with automated refresh cycles and outputs that downstream pipelines could rely on. I also generated country-level maps for the sales team, which they used before contracts were signed.</p></div>
                <div class="card reveal" style="--i:1"><p class="label label--trigger">New perils</p><h3>Water Balance to production, Heat Stress ready</h3><p class="muted">Water Balance was adapted from the Precipitation pipeline and shipped with CI/CD and Docker on Kubernetes. Heat Stress turned a research script into a production-ready pipeline; it was ready at contract end but not deployed during my time.</p></div>
                <div class="card reveal" style="--i:2"><p class="label label--trigger">Dashboards</p><h3>Three Plotly Dash tools used daily</h3><p class="muted">Precipitation, Water Balance and Drought, used every day by the risk and underwriting teams. They were the interface between climate models and underwriting decisions.</p></div>
                <div class="card reveal" style="--i:3"><p class="label label--trigger">Maintenance</p><h3>Drought from early stage to stable</h3><p class="muted">I maintained Precipitation and Drought for two years: calibration checks, geographic extensions, dataset updates. Drought became a stable peril in production across all target countries.</p></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap">
            <div class="section-head reveal"><p class="label label--index">What changed in two years</p><h2>Same team size, measurably wider coverage.</h2></div>
            <div class="table-wrap reveal">
                <table class="data">
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>When I joined · Feb 2024</th><th>When I left · Jan 2026</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>{changes.map(c => <tr><td><strong>{c[0]}</strong></td><td>{c[1]}</td><td>{c[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p class="muted small note">What didn’t change: the perils already in production kept shipping reliably. No regression on Hurricane Wind or Earthquake during the period.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section panel on-panel">
        <div class="wrap insight">
            <p class="label label--trigger">Key insight · the unfinished part</p>
            <h2>Five clients in one country meant five times the same downloads.</h2>
            <p class="lede" style="color:var(--panel-muted)">The architecture was monolithic per client and peril, so identical CHIRPS downloads and calibration runs were repeated for every client. Toward the end of my contract I sketched the alternative: a shared data pool per geography, computed once and refreshed on schedule, wrapped in reusable modules with standard inputs and outputs. New clients in covered geographies become near free to onboard, and compute scales with geographies, not clients.</p>
            <p class="small muted">CHIRPS v2 production ends after December 2026; new rainfall work uses CHIRPS v3.</p>
            <p style="color:var(--panel-muted)">I did not implement that refactor; it would have been a multi-month engagement of its own, out of scope for my contract.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="wrap two">
            <div class="prose">
                <p class="label label--index">How we worked</p>
                <p>Fully embedded in Raincoat’s remote team, time and materials, with weekly check-ins, shared repositories and direct contact with technical leadership and the risk team. Work ran project by project. The overlap between European mornings and American afternoons allowed real-time work without blocking either side.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <p class="label label--index">Stack</p>
                <dl class="fields" style="margin-top:14px">{stack.map(s => <><dt>{s[0]}</dt><dd>{s[1]}</dd></>)}</dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--tint">
        <div class="wrap cta-row">
            <div class="stack" style="--stack:0.8rem">
                <h2>Hitting the same ceilings?</h2>
                <p class="lede">Adding geographies, adding perils, or getting a trigger checked before it pays. Fifteen minutes is enough to see whether I can help.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <a class="btn" href="https://calendly.com/skaraz_data/15-minute-meeting" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-umami-event="book-call-case-study">Book a 15-minute call <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/scope">Start with your trigger</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</Site>

<style>
    .cs-head { padding-block: 40px clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px); }
    .back { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 36px; color: var(--panel-muted); text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.875rem; }
    .cs-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: clamp(28px, 5vw, 64px); align-items: end; }
    .cs-copy { display: grid; gap: 18px; }
    .cs-head h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.1rem + 2.8vw, 3.25rem); }
    .cs-fields { border-top-color: var(--panel-line); }
    .cs-fields dt, .cs-fields dd { border-bottom-color: var(--panel-line); }
    .cs-fields dt { color: var(--panel-muted); }
    .cs-fields dd { color: var(--panel-fg); }
    .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 64px); }
    .two > * { min-width: 0; }
    @media (max-width: 900px) { .cs-grid, .two { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }
    .card { display: grid; gap: 10px; align-content: start; }
    .note { margin-top: 18px; }
    .insight { display: grid; gap: 18px; max-width: 900px; }
    .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; align-items: end; gap: 28px; }
    .row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; }
</style>
